# Per-machine runtime data. These are written continuously by the running desk
# and differ between the Mac mini and the MacBook, so tracking them produced a
# merge conflict on every pull — alpaca_trade_log.json genuinely conflicted on
# 2026-08-06 and had to be resolved by hand. They are observations of what one
# machine did, not shared source.
alpaca_trade_log.json
suggestions.json
claude_suggestions.json
grok_suggestions.json
benchmarks/mention_bursts.jsonl
benchmarks/price_spikes.jsonl
# Rebuildable vendor bars — desk_replay_bench --bars refetches on demand.
benchmarks/replay_bars_cache.json
# Parameter-sweep output, one dated pair per tools/optimize_rstop.py run. It is
# a few hundred KB of every combination tried on one machine's trade log, not a
# result — the conclusion a sweep reaches goes in the commit message that
# changes the config. The 2026-08-16 pair predates this rule and stays tracked.
benchmarks/optimize_rstop_*.json
benchmarks/optimize_rstop_*.csv
# Note: benchmarks/ab_bench_*.csv stays TRACKED — those are A/B results worth
# keeping in history, unlike the raw feeds above.
scratch-pad

# RS screener — ranked output + local universe override. The bar cache is a few
# hundred MB of rebuildable vendor data; the glob matters because WAL mode also
# creates rs_cache.sqlite-wal and -shm.
rs_ratings.json
rs_universe.json
rs_cache.sqlite*

# AI trader + trending screener runtime output (regenerated each loop)
# Prefer ai_* / grok_*; keep claude_* names for older dual-write paths.
claude_suggestions.json
claude_positions_state.json
ai_suggestions.json
ai_positions_state.json
grok_suggestions.json
trending_stocks.json
# Same class as trending_stocks.json: a producer snapshot, rewritten every
# 60s on the mini and meaningless anywhere else. It has a `ts` the seed reads
# to refuse stale data, so a committed copy would be worse than none.
movers_stocks.json

